<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN" "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
Using an externally controlled integer, such as a value parsed from an HTTP query string
parameter, as an array index without first checking that it is within the bounds of the
array allows an attacker to supply a negative or overly large index. In managed C# code this
causes an <code>IndexOutOfRangeException</code>, which can result in a denial of service or
expose error details. When the same index is used in <code>unsafe</code> code or passed to
native code, it can lead to memory corruption.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Before using a user-provided value as an array index, check that it is greater than or equal
to zero and less than the length of the array. For unsigned index types only the upper bound
needs to be checked.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example, an integer is parsed from the <code>id</code> query string parameter
and used directly to write to an array:
</p>
<sample src="ImproperValidationOfArrayIndexBad.cs" />

<p>
The fix is to verify that the index is within <code>0</code> and
<code>array.Length - 1</code> before using it:
</p>
<sample src="ImproperValidationOfArrayIndexGood.cs" />
</example>

<references>
<li>MITRE CWE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/129.html">CWE-129: Improper Validation of Array Index</a>.</li>
<li>Microsoft Learn: <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.indexoutofrangeexception">IndexOutOfRangeException Class</a>.</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
